            "width": 768,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Counts how many times each whole-number label occurs: the count of 0s, of 1s, of 2s, and so on up to the largest label. With weights it totals them instead: the sum of the weights of every element labelled 0, of every element labelled 1, and so on.\n\nLabels come from np.digitize (which zone, which range), np.unique's inverse, a classifier, a list of joint or performer numbers. bincount then gives how often each occurred - or, weighted by speed or energy, how much activity happened in each zone, in one step.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.bincount <length: int>\nDefault: length 0 (just long enough for the largest label).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nlabels:\nWhole numbers, 0 or more (a list is turned into one; any shape is flattened). Every input is counted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nOne weight per label, held. Without them, or if their number does not match, each label counts 1.\n\nlength (int box, also an inlet):\nThe smallest length of the result. Set it to the number of labels there could be, so the result keeps the same length even when the highest labels do not occur.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncounts:\nOne count (or total) per label: position 0 for label 0, and so on.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 0 2 2 1 2 holds one 0, one 1 and three 2s: 1 1 3.\n\nBottom: the same labels weighted by 0.5 1 0.25 2 0.25 - say, speeds in three zones. Zone 0 totals 0.5, zone 1 totals 2, zone 2 totals 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 = 1.5. A metro re-sends the labels twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.unique counts any values, not only small whole numbers. np.histogram counts values falling in ranges. np.digitize turns values into labels.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Counts how many times each whole-number label occurs: the count of 0s, of 1s, of 2s, and so on up to the largest label. With weights it totals them instead: the sum of the weights of every element labelled 0, of every element labelled 1, and so on.\n\nLabels come from np.digitize (which zone, which range), np.unique's inverse, a classifier, a list of joint or performer numbers. bincount then gives how often each occurred - or, weighted by speed or energy, how much activity happened in each zone, in one step.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.bincount <length: int>\nDefault: length 0 (just long enough for the largest label).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nlabels:\nWhole numbers, 0 or more (a list is turned into one; any shape is flattened). Every input is counted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nOne weight per label, held. Without them, or if their number does not match, each label counts 1.\n\nlength (int box, also an inlet):\nThe smallest length of the result. Set it to the number of labels there could be, so the result keeps the same length even when the highest labels do not occur.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncounts:\nOne count (or total) per label: position 0 for label 0, and so on.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: 0 2 2 1 2 holds one 0, one 1 and three 2s: 1 1 3.\n\nBottom: the same labels weighted by 0.5 1 0.25 2 0.25 - say, speeds in three zones. Zone 0 totals 0.5, zone 1 totals 2, zone 2 totals 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 = 1.5. A metro re-sends the labels twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.unique counts any values, not only small whole numbers. np.histogram counts values falling in ranges. np.digitize turns values into labels."
        },
